//==
// # Parameter domains
//
// Layer 0 of the STEP B-rep to mesh design. The parameter domains of curves
// and surfaces in their natural STEP parameters, with periodicity as data: a
// periodic parameter lives on the quotient of the real line by its period
// (README, "A periodic domain as a quotient"). Also the deterministic sampling
// plan that Check_ functions use.
//
// Sections, in order:
//  - Periodicity
//  - Curve and surface domains
//  - Sampling

//==
// # Periodicity

// Whether a parameter wraps, and how. Periodic(Period): the formula wraps, so
// t and t + k * Period name the same point for every integer k and the curve
// can be evaluated at any t (a circle's angle, Period = 2 pi). Closed(Period):
// the formula does not wrap, but the two ends of the range are the same point,
// so the point set is a loop with a seam at the ends while the derivatives
// need not agree there (a clamped B-spline whose first and last control points
// coincide); Period is the range's length, and a parameter is moved into the
// range (Wrap) before evaluation. Layer 3 decides Closed at decode. Seam
// reasoning (wrap, unwrap, winding, edge domains) treats Closed like Periodic;
// IsPeriodic, which says the formula wraps, is false for it. Invariant: Period
// is positive and finite (IsWellFormed), and for Closed it is the range's
// length (Law_ClosedPeriodIsRangeLength).
type Periodicity
    = Aperiodic
    | Periodic(Period: Number)
    | Closed(Period: Number);

//==
// # Curve and surface domains

// The parameter domain of a curve or of one direction of a surface: a closed
// interval of natural parameter values and the parameter's periodicity. For a
// periodic domain the range is one lift of a set on the circle: an edge on a
// circle may have Range [5.5, 7.0] with Period 2 pi. Invariant: Range.Start <=
// Range.End (a range may be a single point; an inverted range is not a
// domain), and the periodicity is well formed. Unbounded ends (a line) are
// allowed; IsProper rejects them.
type CurveDomain
    implements IValue
{
    Range: NumberInterval;
    Periodicity: Periodicity;
}

// The parameter domain of a surface: the product of a U and a V curve domain.
// A cylinder is periodic in U and aperiodic in V; a torus is periodic in both;
// a sphere is periodic in U and has its two V sides collapsed to poles.
type SurfaceDomain
    implements IValue
{
    U: CurveDomain;
    V: CurveDomain;
}

// One side of a surface's parameter rectangle. UMin is the side u = U.Range.Start
// (it runs along v), UMax the side u = U.Range.End, VMin and VMax likewise. A
// seam is a pair of opposite sides identified by periodicity; a pole is a side
// the surface map collapses to one point.
type DomainSide
    = UMin
    | UMax
    | VMin
    | VMax;

//==
// # Sampling

// How many deterministic samples a Check_ takes along one parameter direction:
// Count equally spaced values including both ends of the range. Invariant:
// Count >= 2 (IsWellFormed).
type SamplePlan
    implements IValue
{
    Count: Integer;
}
